The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (!ctx) {
    pr_err("Failed to allocate memory for line info notification\n");
    return NOTIFY_DONE;
}
Reason:
When `kzalloc` fails (ctx is NULL), the error path returns `NOTIFY_DONE` without calling `fput(fp)` to release the file reference previously acquired by `get_file_active()`. This causes a resource leak because the file's reference count remains elevated, preventing proper cleanup.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!ctx) {
    pr_err("Failed to allocate memory for line info notification\n");
    fput(fp);
    return NOTIFY_DONE;
}
Reason:
By adding `fput(fp)` before returning, the file reference is properly decremented and released when memory allocation fails, eliminating the resource leak.

